{-
   What `@lazy` refuses - `doc/spec/functions.md`'s Deferred arguments, and the two waypoints this
   stage has not reached.

   # resolver: ownership

   What a **declaration** is wrong about, which in every case here is that the marker and the
   position describe the same thing differently and one of them would have to be ignored: `&`, `->`
   and an external origin all say something about storage the caller lends, and what arrives at a
   deferred position is a thunk with no storage behind it. A `lens fn` or an `iter fn` is the same
   shape one level up - it resolves its arguments before it hands over, so the argument would run
   whether or not the callee read it.

   `Reject.Force.yana` holds the ones a **body** is wrong about. The two cannot share a file, because
   resolution's error checkpoint is what the body walk runs after - a signature that reported is a
   signature no call is checked against.

   Written in `I32` for `Baseline.yana`'s reason: this stage does not build Core.
-}

fn bump(mark: I32) -> I32 = mark

-- The marker against the conventions. What arrives is the thunk rather than the value, so there is
-- nothing to borrow from and nothing to consume.
fn byRef(@lazy &v: I32) -> I32 = 0

fn sunk(@lazy ->v: I32) -> I32 = 0

-- And against an external origin. The value is made after the call started, so there is no caller
-- origin set for it to be published to.
fn published(@lazy v: 'I32) -> I32 = 0

-- A traversal resolves its arguments before it lifts the rest of the caller's block into the
-- continuation, so a deferred parameter here would be evaluated whether or not it was read.
lens fn scoped(n: I32, @lazy v: I32) -> I32:
    yield n

iter fn walked(n: I32, @lazy v: I32) -> I32:
    yield n

fn main() -> I32 = 0
